```python
def common_element(list1, list2):
    return any(item in list2 for item in list1)
```

This returns `True` on the first shared element and an explicit `False` when there is none, so `assert common_element([1,2,3,4,5], [5,6,7,8,9]) == True` passes. It is O(n·m) in the worst case; `bool(set(list1) & set(list2))` is faster for large or hashable inputs.

